# Datagen fleet configuration - TEMPLATE.
#
# Copy this to job.env (gitignored) and edit it there:
#     Copy-Item tools\cloud\job.env.template tools\cloud\job.env
#
# Only two things have no sensible default and must be set by you:
#     HUB           your Storage Box, as user@host
#     $env:HCLOUD_TOKEN   (an environment variable, not a line in this file -
#                          a Cloud API token does not belong on disk)
#
# Everything below is a working default. Lines marked REPLACE_ME are refused by
# the scripts until you change them.
#
# Sourced by the box scripts (sh) and parsed by the PowerShell drivers, so this
# file is restricted to bare KEY=value lines: no quotes, no shell expansion, no
# spaces around '='. Anything fancier stops being readable from both sides.
#
# A generation is one full pass of this file. Bump GEN, change what you meant to
# change, and rerun; everything else in the pipeline is derived.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- build --
# COMMIT must be a commit that exists on the remote and is CLEAN. The Makefile
# stamps `-dirty` into every shard manifest for a dirty tree, and a dataset
# that cannot be attributed to a build cannot be compared to the next one.
# A private repo must use the ssh form - git@github.com:owner/repo.git - and its
# SSH_KEY_FILE public half must be a read-only deploy key on the repository.
# There are no credentials on a box for an https clone, and git's complaint when
# it wants some ("could not read Username") does not sound like one.
REPO=https://github.com/Stermere/StormBreakerEngine.git
COMMIT=9b9f8d5
ARCH=popcnt
# classical | nnue. Classical needs nothing outside git, which is why it is the
# default: the box build is reproducible from a bare clone. EVAL=nnue makes the
# net a build input, and then NET_SHA below is mandatory.
EVAL=classical
# sha256 of the .nnue to fetch from $HUB/nets/. Required when EVAL=nnue,
# ignored otherwise. Pinning by hash, not by name, is the point.
NET_SHA=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- job --
GEN=gen-001
# label | selfplay
JOB=label
NODES=10000
# auto = nproc. Keep it CONSTANT across restarts of the same chunk: workers
# split their input by line, so a different count reads different lines and
# -resume would then append records for lines it never labelled.
THREADS=auto

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- hub --
# Hetzner Storage Box. Port 23 is not a typo - Storage Boxes serve ssh/scp/rsync
# there, not on 22.
HUB=REPLACE_ME@REPLACE_ME.your-storagebox.de
HUB_PORT=23
HUB_DIR=datagen

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- fleet --
BOXES=4
SERVER_TYPE=cpx62
# The corpus split is I/O bound and needs no engine, so it runs on a cheap
# shared-vCPU box; it needs ~16 GB of disk for the corpus plus its chunks.
# Note the series: cpx11-51 are US-only (ash, hil), cpx12-62 are the EU ones.
PREPARE_TYPE=cpx32
LOCATION=fsn1
IMAGE=ubuntu-24.04
# Name of the key as registered in the Hetzner Cloud console - NOT a path.
SSH_KEY=datagen
# Path to the matching PRIVATE key on this machine. Threaded through every ssh
# and scp call, and installed on each box so it can reach the hub. Leave empty
# to use whatever ssh picks by default.
# Seconds to wait for a new box to answer ssh. The default (900) is sized for a
# freshly allocated address to become routable, which takes minutes on occasion,
# not for the boot, which takes seconds.
SSH_TIMEOUT=900
SSH_KEY_FILE=%USERPROFILE%/.ssh/hetzner_datagen

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ label --
# Source tag stored per record; it is what lets the trainer re-weight the
# mixture with --sources without regenerating anything. Never leave it `other`.
LABEL_SOURCE=human
# Corpus is pre-split into this many chunks on the hub. Keep it a multiple of
# every fleet size you intend to use (8 covers 1/2/4/8) so changing BOXES is a
# config edit rather than a re-split.
CHUNKS=8

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- book --
# sha256 of the opening book to fetch from $HUB/books/. JOB=selfplay draws each
# game's start position from it; empty means games start from the initial
# position, which is what every generation before this one did.
#
# Pinned by hash rather than by name for the same reason NET_SHA is: the book
# decides which positions a generation ever sees, and two books under one
# filename are two datasets that nothing downstream can tell apart.
#
# Publish one with `fleet.ps1 push-book`. To build one - 793,495 unique ply-20
# positions out of the 2.4M-game CCRL 40/40 archive, quiet and within 300cp:
#   tuner extract external/training/ccrl/split_4040/*.pgn -o raw.epd -minply 20 -maxply 20 -stride 1 -maxscore 300
# then strip the result labels and sort -u, because CCRL games share an opening
# book and 58% of the raw lines are repeats of each other.
BOOK_SHA=

# --------------------------------------------------------------- syzygy --
# Which Syzygy set every box fetches and every datagen call is handed; empty
# means none. `3-4-5` is ~939 MB and takes under a minute on a Hetzner link.
#
# Unlike the net and the book this is not pinned by hash and does not go
# through the hub: the tablebases are canonical published data, so what matters
# is not WHICH set but that the set arrived intact. provision.sh checks every
# file against the size the mirror publishes and then runs `datagen syzygy`,
# which probes known endgames with the binary that box just built - because a
# box with half a set does not fail, it quietly labels low-piece positions from
# the search and its records are indistinguishable from everyone else's.
#
# Both halves are fetched. WDL answers won/drawn/lost inside the search; DTZ is
# what converts at the root under the fifty-move rule, and without it a
# labelling search of a five-man position falls back to the evaluation, which
# is the bug the tables exist to fix.
SYZYGY=

# Where from; the default is the lichess.org mirror.
# SYZYGY_URL=https://tablebase.lichess.ovh/tables/standard

# --------------------------------------------------------------- selfplay --
# Games per batch, and batches in total across the fleet. Batches exist because
# `selfplay` has no -resume: a rerun truncates the shard and starts over, so an
# interruption costs one batch instead of the whole run.
SELFPLAY_GAMES=55000
SELFPLAY_BATCHES=8
# Batch b uses seed SEED_BASE + b*SEED_STRIDE. The stride is large and prime so
# that streams from different batches cannot interleave: worker k of a run
# derives seed + k*0x9E3779B97F4A7C15, and adjacent integer seeds would put two
# batches' workers one draw apart. Never let two batches share a seed - they
# produce byte-identical shards and nothing downstream would notice.
SELFPLAY_SEED_BASE=1000
SELFPLAY_SEED_STRIDE=1000003
# Random plies played out of the book (off the start position when there is no
# book). This is the ONLY variation two games ever get: after it the engine
# plays fixed-node searches on one thread, which is deterministic, so a game is
# a function of its start position and these plies and nothing else.
#
# Out of a deep book that makes it a perturbation, and it wants to be small.
# Two games sharing a book line and differing by a couple of plies then play out
# from near-identical positions, so the difference in their RESULTS is
# attributable to the perturbation rather than to noise - which is the only
# thing that makes the trainer's game-result term (its lambda) worth anything.
# Long random openings destroy that: the games no longer share a position.
#
# Empty for datagen's own default, which is 2 with a book and 8 without.
#
# `MIN-MAX` draws the count per game, and out of a book it wants to be a range
# rather than a number: a book extracted at one ply is one side to move on every
# line of it, so a fixed EVEN count starts every game in the generation with
# white to move and a fixed odd one with black. 2-3 splits it. The manifest
# records both bounds.
SELFPLAY_OPENING=2-3
# Interior search-tree nodes reservoir-sampled per played move. 0 is game line
# only, which is also datagen's default.
SELFPLAY_TREE=0
# Discard a game whose opening is already decided by more than this many cp. A
# balanced book rarely trips it; an unbalanced one (UHO and friends) trips it
# constantly and throws away most of what the fleet is billed for, so raise it
# if you change to one.
SELFPLAY_OPENING_SCORE=800
